  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 204 AM CDT Tue Oct 20 2026\n\nA stable omega blocking pattern becomes firmly established across \nthe central United States today, keeping our region under the \ncontrol of an upper-level ridge. Kept the trend for slightly \nincreased daily maximum temperatures going each day through \nThursday. Plentiful sunshine will prevail alongside light southerly \nbreezes. Meanwhile, local lake breezes will develop along the Lake \nSuperior shoreline during the afternoon, keeping coastal areas \nnoticeably cooler and closer to seasonal norms along the North \nShore, while interior communities continue to experience \nunseasonable autumn warmth. The lake breeze is expected to move the \nmost inland on Tuesday and Thursday. There will be a slight ripple \nin the blocking pattern for tomorrow as a weak cold front and upper \nlevel trough crosses the Upper Midwest. Not expecting these features \nto amount too much aside from a slight increase in clouds for \nTuesday night through Wednesday morning before dry air aloft \noverspreads the region again. \n\nChanges begin on Friday as the persistent omega ridge starts to \nbreak down. A closed upper-level low over the Great Basin will eject \nnortheastward toward the Upper Midwest. Cloud cover will steadily \nincrease throughout the day. Rain chances are still on track to \narrive Friday afternoon into Saturday, and confidence is higher that \nprecipitation will occur over that time period even if scattered as \nmodels are becoming more constant with that timing. Because \ntemperatures ahead of the system remain unseasonably mild, all \nprecipitation will fall strictly as liquid rain.\n\nScattered light rain showers will linger across the area through \nSaturday as the trough axis and associated cold front push across \nthe Upper Great Lakes. By Monday, dry conditions return as high \npressure rebuilds behind the departing system, accompanied by \nclearing skies. A significant air mass change behind the cold front \nwill drop temperatures back toward seasonal values for late\nOctober.",
